00:25:42.060 --> 00:25:44.340
So you might ask, okay, Percy,

00:25:44.700 --> 00:25:46.880
What's the grand challenge you're bringing to

00:25:46.880 --> 00:25:48.740
me? In 2026?

00:25:49.860 --> 00:25:51.559
And to answer this question,

00:25:52.550 --> 00:25:54.709
I think we have to step back and kind

00:25:54.710 --> 00:25:56.849
of reframe things a little bit.

00:25:57.490 --> 00:25:59.570
So iCLEAR, as you would all agree,

00:25:59.570 --> 00:26:01.030
is a machine learning conference.

00:26:02.400 --> 00:26:04.340
So what do machine learning

00:26:04.480 --> 00:26:06.609
researchers do? So where the

00:26:06.610 --> 00:26:08.230
core machine learning researcher

00:26:08.770 --> 00:26:10.730
does is create better algorithms.

00:26:11.340 --> 00:26:13.419
And what is the learning algorithm? It's, something

00:26:13.420 --> 00:26:15.730
that takes training data and produces

00:26:15.790 --> 00:26:17.800
a model. So the key question that a

00:26:17.800 --> 00:26:19.750
machine learning researcher has to face is,

00:26:19.990 --> 00:26:22.059
is my algorithm producing a model that

00:26:22.060 --> 00:26:23.900
generalizes from the training data?

00:26:24.140 --> 00:26:26.320
To the test data? So this is machine

00:26:26.320 --> 00:26:28.619
learning one zero one. So you're, like, Percy, why

00:26:28.620 --> 00:26:30.059
are you bothering me with this?

00:26:31.180 --> 00:26:32.710
Because I think there's often,

00:26:34.290 --> 00:26:36.520
kind of a confusion here, which is that

00:26:36.600 --> 00:26:38.820
there's also another goal. Which is

00:26:38.820 --> 00:26:40.860
if you're a model developer, your

00:26:40.860 --> 00:26:43.020
goal is to produce better models, and a model is something

00:26:43.020 --> 00:26:44.929
that takes the input and produces an output.

00:26:45.490 --> 00:26:47.570
So here the key question is, how will this

00:26:47.570 --> 00:26:49.600
model work? This is

00:26:49.600 --> 00:26:51.760
very useful and we all benefit from

00:26:51.760 --> 00:26:53.780
having strong models But

00:26:53.780 --> 00:26:55.820
this is a different question than the

00:26:55.820 --> 00:26:56.800
one about generalization.

00:26:58.040 --> 00:27:00.330
And I think often we somehow

00:27:00.850 --> 00:27:02.930
know, don't, make a difference between

00:27:02.930 --> 00:27:04.630
these two, and that lens through a lot of

00:27:05.110 --> 00:27:07.159
confusion. So how are these

00:27:07.159 --> 00:27:09.160
two things related? Well, there's

00:27:09.160 --> 00:27:11.040
a very simple equation that relates them.

00:27:11.840 --> 00:27:13.780
So as a model developer,

00:27:14.330 --> 00:27:16.669
your goal is to produce better algorithms,

00:27:16.930 --> 00:27:18.980
to measure how good your algorithm is We're

00:27:18.980 --> 00:27:21.240
gonna use something called, algorithmic efficiency,

00:27:21.809 --> 00:27:23.510
could either be computer data efficiency

00:27:24.680 --> 00:27:26.220
If you're a model developer,

00:27:26.880 --> 00:27:29.220
then your goal is to push up the end accuracy.

00:27:31.179 --> 00:27:33.419
And if you're a fundraiser, your goal is

00:27:33.419 --> 00:27:35.260
to push up the amount of resources you have.

00:27:36.140 --> 00:27:38.380
So hopefully, this makes it absolutely clear what

00:27:38.380 --> 00:27:40.440
everyone's role in in life

00:27:40.440 --> 00:27:42.789
is. Okay.

00:27:42.789 --> 00:27:44.420
So now since

00:27:44.799 --> 00:27:46.990
this is an iclear talk, we're gonna

00:27:46.990 --> 00:27:49.490
focus on the middle part which is how do we improve

00:27:49.510 --> 00:27:50.615
algorithmic efficiency.

00:27:51.679 --> 00:27:53.949
And this question has actually been

00:27:54.189 --> 00:27:56.509
pretty sharply studied by a bunch of

00:27:56.510 --> 00:27:58.919
of papers, There's this actually a very

00:27:58.919 --> 00:28:01.220
early open AI paper that looked at,

00:28:01.380 --> 00:28:03.790
in the context of image classification,

00:28:04.600 --> 00:28:06.680
that would make efficiency of neural nets increased by

00:28:06.680 --> 00:28:08.620
a factor of 44 x over

00:28:08.860 --> 00:28:11.130
seven years. More recent

00:28:11.130 --> 00:28:12.900
work by epoch AI showed that

00:28:13.460 --> 00:28:15.880
every eight months the algorithmic efficiency has

00:28:16.050 --> 00:28:17.075
been doubling.

00:28:18.299 --> 00:28:20.160
So there has been a lot of

00:28:20.970 --> 00:28:22.630
progress. And in particular,

00:28:23.350 --> 00:28:25.660
there's a lot of progress in the

00:28:25.660 --> 00:28:26.310
frontier labs.

00:28:29.290 --> 00:28:31.300
And there's also been a lot of progress

00:28:31.780 --> 00:28:33.059
in the open community.

00:28:34.420 --> 00:28:36.580
But, you know, how how can we make this

00:28:36.580 --> 00:28:38.610
even better? So we can make

00:28:38.610 --> 00:28:40.719
three wishes. This

00:28:40.720 --> 00:28:42.310
is what I will wish for. So

00:28:42.850 --> 00:28:44.850
which one is

00:28:44.980 --> 00:28:47.419
open development? So we all write papers.

00:28:47.480 --> 00:28:49.790
What are papers? There's this nice glossy

00:28:50.010 --> 00:28:52.257
PDF files that we upload to archive. They

00:28:52.257 --> 00:28:54.470
have nice introductions. Lots of

00:28:54.470 --> 00:28:56.890
citations, some beautiful figures.

00:28:57.820 --> 00:28:59.926
And this is great, but we

00:28:59.926 --> 00:29:01.710
all know that underneath

00:29:01.929 --> 00:29:04.090
the surface, is a very messy nonlinear

00:29:04.550 --> 00:29:06.850
research process that contains tons of failed

00:29:06.850 --> 00:29:08.870
experiments, and all of these hallway

00:29:08.870 --> 00:29:11.028
conversations, the rationales for why things were done

00:29:11.029 --> 00:29:13.039
in a certain way. And that

00:29:13.039 --> 00:29:15.100
is a valuable part of the

00:29:15.100 --> 00:29:17.200
science, but it rarely gets

00:29:17.695 --> 00:29:18.150
shown.

00:29:19.910 --> 00:29:21.770
And so my call

00:29:22.250 --> 00:29:24.410
is for open development to surface all of

00:29:24.410 --> 00:29:26.330
these things because that is

00:29:26.470 --> 00:29:28.679
ultimately ground truth. The paper

00:29:28.679 --> 00:29:30.700
is just a projection of that ground truth

00:29:31.100 --> 00:29:33.039
onto something that will satisfy

00:29:33.179 --> 00:29:35.500
Rivera too. So speaking of reviewer

00:29:35.640 --> 00:29:37.430
two, my second wish is

00:29:37.750 --> 00:29:39.200
for algorithms at scale.

00:29:39.919 --> 00:29:41.740
So last year, I actually got a review

00:29:42.380 --> 00:29:44.530
where the reviewer asked, why don't you try

00:29:44.530 --> 00:29:47.320
this pretrained experiment on 70,000,000,000

00:29:47.380 --> 00:29:49.500
parameter models? Thank you, Roberto,

00:29:49.640 --> 00:29:50.860
for your insightful comments.

00:29:53.120 --> 00:29:54.649
Well, so there's a tension here

00:29:55.530 --> 00:29:57.670
So obviously, most of us don't

00:29:57.910 --> 00:29:59.990
have enough resources to run things at large

00:29:59.990 --> 00:30:02.270
scale. But this is in tension

00:30:02.570 --> 00:30:04.820
with algorithms at small scale don't

00:30:04.820 --> 00:30:06.359
always work at large scale.

00:30:07.720 --> 00:30:09.740
And so what I want to hopefully

00:30:09.799 --> 00:30:11.790
convey and convince you of

00:30:12.190 --> 00:30:14.610
is the power of of scaling loss.

00:30:16.370 --> 00:30:18.790
So my third wish is for predictability.

00:30:20.210 --> 00:30:22.290
So our stated goal is improve learning

00:30:22.290 --> 00:30:24.770
algorithms. But a stronger

00:30:24.770 --> 00:30:26.870
goal would be to have an actual scientific

00:30:27.510 --> 00:30:29.760
theory or understanding of what makes

00:30:29.760 --> 00:30:32.240
a learning algorithm good. So for example,

00:30:32.460 --> 00:30:34.700
if you understand the laws of physics, you can plan

00:30:34.840 --> 00:30:36.850
predict planetary motion. You won't have to

00:30:36.850 --> 00:30:38.770
run a bunch of I don't even know what experiments,

00:30:39.750 --> 00:30:40.650
that case would be.

00:30:42.540 --> 00:30:44.910
And and so one

00:30:45.289 --> 00:30:47.500
way to operationalize the notion of understanding, which

00:30:47.500 --> 00:30:49.920
is know, kind of a vague, is

00:30:50.120 --> 00:30:52.390
if you can predict the outcome of an experiment

00:30:52.390 --> 00:30:53.479
before you run it.

00:30:54.520 --> 00:30:56.860
And in many areas of science, this is called preregistration.

00:30:57.530 --> 00:30:59.640
You call your shots before you actually do the

00:30:59.640 --> 00:31:01.730
experiment. And I'm going to also talk

00:31:01.730 --> 00:31:03.440
about how we should do more of this.

00:31:04.880 --> 00:31:07.220
So I'm gonna talk about all three wishes

