01:22:28.240 --> 01:22:30.020
Then again, it's kind of like

01:22:31.620 --> 01:22:33.840
clinical trials where the things that are

01:22:33.840 --> 01:22:35.989
obviously bad we just don't.

01:22:36.630 --> 01:22:38.390
You know. That that's feedback.

01:22:39.010 --> 01:22:41.160
Go back to the drawing board. Things

01:22:41.160 --> 01:22:43.299
that are promising we kind of run at the next,

01:22:43.700 --> 01:22:46.020
scale and so on and so forth. Now

01:22:46.640 --> 01:22:49.110
the challenge actually with a thousand different

01:22:49.110 --> 01:22:51.280
things is that you how

01:22:51.280 --> 01:22:53.300
do you combine them? And when

01:22:53.300 --> 01:22:55.320
you combine them, do they stack?

01:22:55.590 --> 01:22:57.850
Or do they interfere with each other? And

01:22:57.910 --> 01:22:59.990
that's another dimension which we haven't

01:22:59.990 --> 01:23:01.999
had to really address

01:23:02.220 --> 01:23:04.599
yet because you know, most of our development is fairly

01:23:04.599 --> 01:23:05.820
serial. But if you have

01:23:06.810 --> 01:23:09.130
you know, I I think large organizations that are developing

01:23:09.130 --> 01:23:11.030
models have this problem all the time.

01:23:11.190 --> 01:23:12.720
In which case you need to

01:23:14.390 --> 01:23:16.550
you know, exercise judgment. But I think that's

01:23:16.550 --> 01:23:18.800
also a sort of meta research problem

01:23:19.280 --> 01:23:21.070
how to combine different,

01:23:21.420 --> 01:23:22.450
you know, ideas and

01:23:23.729 --> 01:23:25.760
make them compatible. Example,

01:23:25.760 --> 01:23:27.800
you can sample a bunch of random things

01:23:27.800 --> 01:23:29.930
and run tournaments. But

